          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Educational Path</strong><br>\nGertrude Belle Elion was born on January 23, 1918, in New York City. She studied chemistry at Hunter College and completed her graduate work at New York University. Elion overcame the barriers faced by women in science at the time to build a career in biochemical research.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Breakthroughs in Medicinal Chemistry</strong><br>\nElion's innovative research methods led to the development of new drugs for treating leukemia, autoimmune disorders, urinary tract infections, gout, malaria, and viral herpes. Her work was instrumental in introducing treatments that extended lives and improved the quality of life for many patients.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize Achievement</strong><br>\nIn 1988, Elion shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with George H. Hitchings and Sir James Black for their use of innovative research methods to develop drugs deemed critical for treating several major diseases.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
